Pre-existing, not introduced by karr #37; found while reviewing its AAD change and reproduced against the tree without that change.

_is_comment_leaf gates its wire half on `defined $data_key`, and the comment above it says why: without the gate, a caller plain string that happens to spell ENC[...,type:comment] would be dropped from the digest on the read side while _encrypt_tree wrote it as an ordinary type:str value. That reasoning covers the ENCRYPTED slot, and there the round trip is fine (verified).

It does not cover the slot the encryption rule EXCLUDES. There the literal string reaches the document verbatim, so on the way back in it IS an ENC[...,type:comment] token: _digested_leaves drops it from the digest, where the encrypt side hashed its text. The document fails its own MAC.

    File::SOPS->encrypt(
        data => { db_unencrypted => { q{} => [ $literal_enc_comment_string ] },
                  k => q{v} },
        recipients => [$pub], format => q{yaml},
    );

Written at exit 0; decrypt then croaks. Today the croak comes from the leaf ("Authentication failed") rather than from the MAC, because _decrypt_tree reaches the token first and tries to decrypt it -- so the symptom is louder than a MAC mismatch and the outcome is not silent. That is why this is low priority rather than a defect that ships wrong data. Still: a file this library writes and cannot read back.

Same shape as the guard _encrypt_tree already has for a comment in a mapping value slot (docs/adr/0041). The candidate fix is the twin of that one -- refuse a caller string that parses as type:comment in a slot the rule excludes -- and belongs with whoever revisits ADR 0041/0046. No AAD or wire byte moves either way.
